    {/* Provenance / Novelty */}
    <section id="novelty" class="border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]/60 p-5 space-y-3 scroll-mt-20">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[color:var(--color-signal-green)]">
        ▸ Provenance / novelty detection
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        For each PURSUE page, we run cosine top-1 similarity against a
        reference embedding index of prior public-archive UAP material. The
        top-1 score per page rolls up to a card-level{" "}
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">disclosure_status</code>{" "}
        of <em>novel</em>, <em>partial</em>, or <em>previously-disclosed</em>.
        Both sides are embedded with the same model
        (<code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">voyage-3</code>,
        1024d) so cosines are comparable.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-xs font-mono border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-[color:var(--color-border-bright)] text-[color:var(--color-text-faint)] uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-[10px]">
              <th class="text-left py-2 pr-4">Status</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 pr-4">Rule</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2">Interpretation</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
            <tr class="border-b border-[color:var(--color-border)]">
              <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-[color:var(--color-signal-green)]">novel</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">&gt;70% of pages have top-1 cosine &lt; 0.70</td>
              <td class="py-2 text-[color:var(--color-text-dim)]">No close prior-disclosure match found</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-[color:var(--color-border)]">
              <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-[color:var(--color-signal-amber)]">partial</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">mixed — neither rule above triggers</td>
              <td class="py-2 text-[color:var(--color-text-dim)]">Some pages overlap with prior material; some don't</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">previously-disclosed</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">&gt;70% of pages have top-1 cosine ≥ 0.85</td>
              <td class="py-2 text-[color:var(--color-text-dim)]">Substantial overlap with material in the reference corpus</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        The 0.85 / 0.70 thresholds are starting points. They'll be tuned
        against a manual spot-check of 50 random pages once the real
        Black Vault reference corpus lands and the noise floor is
        empirically known.
      </p>
      <h3 class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)] pt-2">
        Reference corpora
      </h3>
      <ul class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)] space-y-2 pl-5 list-disc marker:text-[color:var(--color-text-faint)]">
        <li>
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-amber)] font-semibold">v1 launch — placeholder.</span>{" "}
          The currently-integrated reference corpus is a small synthetic
          placeholder of 10 hand-crafted public-domain UFO-adjacent text
          passages (Roswell 1947 press releases, Project Blue Book final
          report, Hottel memo, RB-47 1957, Malmstrom 1967, Condon Report
          1969, AAWSAP 2017). This is a <em>methodology demo</em>, not a
          real coverage measurement. Card pages whose top-1 match scores
          high against this placeholder are pages that semantically resemble
          well-known prior-disclosure material — but the absence of a match
          here does <em>not</em> mean the page is genuinely new, only that
          it doesn't resemble these 10 passages.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)]">Post-launch — Black Vault.</span>{" "}
          The novelty-detection plan calls for the Black Vault as the v1
          real reference (the canonical prior-disclosure FOIA archive on
          the order of 100k–500k pages). Acquiring + OCR'ing + embedding
          the bulk archive is a separate operational task. Once integrated,
          every existing card automatically gets a meaningful disclosure
          status without any user-facing change.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)]">Backlog.</span>{" "}
          NICAP case files; AAWSAP/AATIP leaked materials (tagged
          separately as "leaked, not officially declassified"); CIA
          CREST records that touch UAP topics; Project Blue Book archive.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        The card detail page surfaces a Provenance panel showing the
        top-3 closest reference matches with cosine score, reference
        archive id, and source document id. Every "previously-disclosed"
        tag is therefore receipt-backed — no invisible algorithmic
        verdicts.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[color:var(--color-text-faint)] pt-2">
